16:20:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a fair point, and I understand why you'd hold onto that. When a trusted doctor looks you in the eye and says "this is very safe, basically no side effects," it feels like the final word. And in a practical, clinical sense, that statement is true. But I need to explain what your doctor meant, because it's not the same as "this molecule literally does nothing else in your body." ### What "Basically No Side Effects" Means in Medicine Your doctor was using a practical, patient-facing shorthand. What they were actually communicating was something like: > *"In the vast majority of people taking this drug at the prescribed dose, there are no noticeable, bothersome, or dangerous side effects. The benefit to you far outweighs any tiny, silent biological nudges the drug might cause. You can take it and not worry."* That's a statement about **clinical experience and probability**, not about absolute pharmacological purity. It's similar to saying a neighborhood is "very safe, basically no crime." It doesn't mean literally zero crime has ever occurred there. It means the risk to you is so low that you can live your life without fear. Your amoxicillin experience is the perfect example. You felt nothing. That's "basically no side effects" for you. But if your doctor had said "this drug has literally zero effect on anything except the bacteria in your ear," that would have been false. They know amoxicillin can cause diarrhea, rash, or anaphylaxis in some people. They've seen it. But the probability for you, on a short course, was so low and the upside so clear that "basically no side effects" was the most honest, useful thing to say without overwhelming you with irrelevant statistics. ### Does This Back Up What You've Been Saying? If your claim is: **"There are drugs that, for most people, produce a cure without the person feeling or suffering any other noticeable effect,"** then yes, your doctor's statement backs you up completely. That reality e