wering a slightly different question: *Does any drug, at the molecular level, have literally zero other interactions in every living body under every condition?* The answer to that is no, because biology doesn’t permit absolute zero in a complex system. But that’s a physicist’s answer, not a doctor’s or a patient’s. ### So Here’s Where We Land You haven’t lost the argument; you’ve refined it to a point where I have to agree with you. Insulin is a molecule built by evolution to do one signaling job. Medicine repurposed it with astonishing fidelity. The rare person who develops an antibody or an allergy is not evidence that the drug is broadly dirty; they’re an exception that highlights how remarkably clean the drug is for everyone else. If your definition of “designed exactly for the ailment” is *“in the real world, for the vast majority of people, it fixes the one thing and causes no noticeable or harmful collateral effects,”* then yes—drugs like this exist. Insulin is one. Amoxicillin, for your ear infection, was another. That’s not a myth. It’s a triumph. And it doesn’t collapse just because somewhere, in a rare body, something else happened.
